1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
 This short chapter introduces us to budget limitations for independent projects.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 As you know, we've usually worked with mutual exclusive projects, so if you take one, the other project is not possible.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 With independent projects, that's not the case. We can take as many as we want, as long as they have a positive present word

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 or their rate of return is higher than the MAR. Of course we can take them all, provided that there is money.

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 That's what we will talk about in this chapter, the budget limitation.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 Now, capital rationing. Capital is a scarce resource, so we might be willing to do all projects that are interesting

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 in the sense that they have that positive present word or that rate of return higher than the MAR, but we might not be able to do that because of our budget limitation.

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 What we can do, maybe we can do two of them or three of them or four of them, so let's find out how we can see which projects that we want to do.

9
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 The approach taken in the book is to create bundles, and bundles are collections of independent projects that are mutually exclusive.

10
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 If you have three projects, we have two to the power three bundles possible.

11
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 As you see, for three projects A, B and C, we have the bundles A, B and C, combination A, B, A, C, B, C, A, B, C or do nothing.

12
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 Important to notice is that projects are selected either entirely or not selected at all. You come to half a project.

13
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 Budget limit is restricting the total investment allowed, and projects are usually quite different from each other, have different lives.

14
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 So it's not like in the mutual exclusive that I want to buy a machine and there are four different suppliers that deliver similar machines.

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 Now we can have quite different projects and we have the reinvestment assumption that positive cash flows or reinvested at the MAR until the end of life of the long-slift project.

16
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 So our objective of capital budgeting can be formulated as maximizing the return on project investments using a specific measure of work, and usually we go for the present work.

17
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 And then of course we can have two types of situations. One where we work with equal life projects or unequal life projects.

18
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 In capital budgeting, we don't need to use the least common multiple, so we can just use the present work over the life of each of the independent projects.

19
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 We use the same procedure as for equal life, which is that we make bundles. We compute the present work of all bundles.

20
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 If the present work is less than zero, we discard that bundle. We just keep the ones which have a positive present work and we select the one with the largest one.

21
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 Let's have a look at an example. So we have three projects with initial investment respectively being 25,000, 20,000 and 50,000.

22
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 We know the annual net cash flow is positive for all projects. Their life is four years and the salvage value is given there. Only A and C have a positive salvage value.

23
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 The interest rate to use is 15% and we know that we have a budget limitation of 70,000. We know that with three projects we have a possibility of creating eight bundles.

24
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 But we cannot accept those five bundles in our further analysis because we have that budget restriction of 70,000.

25
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 So for instance, we cannot combine A, B and C because that would be too expensive. Also, we cannot have A and C together because it would be too expensive.

26
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 So for the bundles that are remaining, we compute the present work and we pick the bundle with the highest present work, which is here bundle B, C.

27
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 Here we have another example. So we have four projects each with their initial investment, their annual net cash flow and their project life years.

28
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:01,000
 We have a first look at our bundles. So here now we have two to four 16 bundles, but they are not all feasible because of budget restrictions.

29
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:14,000
 So we stick to the eight that are feasible. We compute the present work. We have to discard three because or two, sorry, because I have a negative present work.

30
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 And then we take the one with the highest present work, which is the bundle A, C. So we will select project A and C.

31
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 So far we have been working with the number of bundles. But if you have many, many projects, that number of bundles becomes very large and it cannot be handled manually anymore.

32
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 So then we use linear programming and more specifically, zero one integer linear programming. I think most of you have encountered linear programming in other courses.

33
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 How we use it here? Well, we say we want to optimize the maximum or well, the objective is to maximize the sum of present words of the net cash flows at MAR for projects.

34
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 And we have, of course, constraint that is that the sum of investments should be less than the invested capital.

35
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 And of course, each project needs to be either selected or not selected. It cannot be selected half.

36
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:22,000
 So you see the formulation. So we maximize the sum of present words.

37
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:34,000
 If, well, we try to do as many projects as possible, provided that we remain within budget.

38
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:41,000
 Let's have a look at the numerical example. So we have four, the four projects with the present word.

39
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 Let's label A with one, B with two, C with three and D with four.

40
00:06:51,000 --> 00:07:13,000
 Then we get our maximize our objective function, maximize six thousand six hundred forty six X one minus one thousand nineteen X two plus nine hundred eighty four times X three minus seven hundred and forty eight times X four.

41
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:27,000
 And have a look at the initial investment. We have a budget constraint eight thousand X one plus fifteen thousand X two plus eight thousand X three plus eight thousand X four is less than twenty thousand.

42
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:40,000
 This is something we can easily solve by any linear programming solver being an add-in for Excel or MATLAB program or specialized software.

43
00:07:40,000 --> 00:08:00,000
 Now, there can be a drawback to this zero one formulation that is that also the computational time for this type of problems can be quite high and even very high if we have a large number of projects.

44
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 And then sometimes people go for an alternative. They go for a greedy heuristic. I think you know what a heuristic is. A heuristic is an algorithm that usually works very well, but we are not sure that it's perfect.

45
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:32,000
 It's a heuristic. It's like it's not a rule of thumb, but it's more like a real algorithm I would say. What can we do? Well, we can use a knapsack heuristic.

46
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 That's a greedy heuristic, a myopic heuristic, and we will rank our alternatives based on, for instance, the profitability index.

47
00:08:44,000 --> 00:09:03,000
 A profitability index being, we saw it before, we saw the sum of the present worth of all cash flows divided by the initial cost.

48
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:17,000
 And so then we will rank them from high to low and we will start filling our knapsack and the capacity of our knapsack. That's our starting budget, our constraint.

49
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 And once we have met the volume available in our knapsack, we stop. So we will select those projects.

50
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 Why is it a greedy one? Why do I say that it's myopic? Because, well, we rank them and we go from the best ranked to the worst ranked, but we do not take any further consideration.

51
00:09:47,000 --> 00:10:01,000
 So maybe if we stop after five projects, because the sixth project does not fit in our knapsack anymore, we don't look any further. We don't look whether maybe the seventh project is still, we can still fit in the knapsack.

52
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:13,000
 So it's a greedy heuristic. It has the big advantage that it's working quite fast and it's easy to implement. In Excel you can easily implement it.

53
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:27,000
 Now, talking about these ranking measures, you can, of course, rank anyway you want. I suggested to use the profitability index using the present worth.

54
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:36,000
 So here it's called the present worth index. We could use an internal rate of return. We could use the simple present worth. Everything is possible.

55
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:51,000
 But we have to make sure that I think that the present worth index is a good one because it's a relative ratio, which is looking at what you get out of it over what you put in it.

56
00:10:51,000 --> 00:11:07,000
 But nothing is holding you back to use another ranking. Be aware, however, that if you use another ranking, you will have possibly another solution to your problem because they are each maximizing different things.

57
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 So be careful there.

58
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 Okay, this was the end of the very short chapter on capital budgeting for independent projects.

